<clickhouse>
    <!-- TTL overrides for the system observability tables that retain rows for 30 days by default
         and balloon to hundreds of MB under fuzz pressure. ClickHouse's system_log config supports
         a <ttl> element that the server applies on startup when (re)creating the table, so this
         override is durable across container restarts. ALTER TABLE ... MODIFY TTL on a system log
         table is non-durable: the server can reapply the config-defined engine on the next start
         and the ALTER is lost (observed on processors_profile_log on 26.5.1.805; trace_log was
         spared because <trace_log remove="remove"/> short-circuits the recreate path).

         text_log and query_log retain their defaults: they are the canonical inputs for triaging
         a sqlancer-found bug (which query failed, which thread fired which statement), so a tight
         TTL would shrink the diagnostic window. processors_profile_log is purely pipeline-shape
         observability, which is uninteresting for sqlancer dev iterations.

         processors_profile_log: 30 day default -> 1 hour. -->
    <processors_profile_log>
        <ttl>event_date + INTERVAL 1 HOUR DELETE</ttl>
    </processors_profile_log>
</clickhouse>
